  "content": {
    "id": "p20-q4-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "Find all rational roots of $P(x)=2x^3+3x-5$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x=1$ only.",
      "worked": "By the Rational Root Theorem, the possible rational roots are $\\pm1,\\pm5,\\pm\\frac12,\\pm\\frac52$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}P(1)&=2+3-5=0\\\\P(-1)&=-2-3-5=-10\\ne0\\\\P(5)&=250+15-5=260\\ne0\\\\P(-5)&=-250-15-5=-270\\ne0\\\\P\\left(\\frac12\\right)&=\\frac14+\\frac32-5=-\\frac{13}{4}\\ne0\\\\P\\left(-\\frac12\\right)&=-\\frac14-\\frac32-5=-\\frac{27}{4}\\ne0\\\\P\\left(\\frac52\\right)&=\\frac{125}{4}+\\frac{15}{2}-5=\\frac{135}{4}\\ne0\\\\P\\left(-\\frac52\\right)&=-\\frac{125}{4}-\\frac{15}{2}-5=-\\frac{175}{4}\\ne0\\end{align*}$$\n\nTherefore, the only rational root is $x=1$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 90
  },
